# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# faster-than-light-illusions (Miya's Note). Script from miya 2026-11-10 (dated 2026-10-24); fact-checked per guideline 12-1 (see script-check.md).
SCENES = [
 ('S01', 'Flip the Switch', 'ill', 'p01a', 'Tonight, before you go to bed, try flipping a light switch in a dark room. The darkness vanishes so swiftly that your eyes perceive no delay at all. In that single flick of a finger, countless packets of electromagnetic energy bounce off every surface, illuminating your bedroom in mere billionths of a second.'),
 ('S02', 'Flip the Switch', 'ill', 'p01b', 'We grow up believing that light represents the absolute pinnacle of speed in our universe. Yet modern physics tells us that light in a vacuum travels at a very specific number: about 299,792 kilometers per second.'),
 ('S03', 'The Cosmic Speed Limit', 'ill', 'p02a', 'For more than a century, Albert Einstein and the framework of modern physics have taught us a strict cosmic rule. Nothing can travel faster than this speed, often symbolized simply as the letter c.'),
 ('S04', 'The Cosmic Speed Limit', 'ill', 'p02b', 'It is not merely a technical limitation of our current technology, but a fundamental barrier woven into the fabric of space and time. Accelerating any physical object with mass to this speed would require an infinite amount of energy.'),
 ('S05', 'What the Limit Means', 'ill', 'p03a', 'Before we test that rule, we must ask what this speed limit actually means. In physics, it is fundamentally the speed of causality itself. Causality is the simple, unbreakable rule that a cause must always happen before its effect. If information could travel infinitely fast, the logical order of events across our universe would instantly dissolve into chaos.'),
 ('S06', 'The Laser Pointer', 'ill', 'p04a', 'Imagine you are standing on your rooftop on a clear night, looking up at the full Moon. The Moon sits roughly 380,000 kilometers away from where you are standing. Now, imagine you hold a laser pointer capable of casting an intensely bright red dot onto the lunar surface.'),
 ('S07', 'The Laser Pointer', 'chalk', 'p04b', 'If you hold your hand steady, the light takes about 1.3 seconds to travel from your roof to the dusty craters of the Moon. But what happens if you suddenly flick your wrist and sweep the beam from one side of the lunar disk to the other?'),
 ('S08', 'A Racing Dot', 'ill', 'p05a', 'If you sweep your hand across an angle of about half a degree in a fraction of a second, that tiny red dot will race across the entire face of the Moon.'),
 ('S09', 'A Racing Dot', 'ill', 'p05b', 'Because the Moon is thousands of kilometers wide, a swift turn of your wrist causes the dot to travel across the lunar terrain at speeds exceeding hundreds of thousands of kilometers per second.'),
 ('S10', 'A Racing Dot', 'ill', 'p05c', 'If you performed this same thought experiment on a screen placed millions of kilometers away, the illuminated dot would easily sweep across the surface faster than 299,792 kilometers per second. Has your laser pointer just broken the ultimate law of physics?'),
 ('S11', 'An Optical Illusion', 'ill', 'p06a', 'The truth is that the red dot is an optical illusion of motion. The dot on the left side of the Moon and the dot on the right side are not the same physical entity. When you shine a laser, you are not waving a rigid stick of light across space.'),
 ('S12', 'An Optical Illusion', 'ill', 'p06b', 'You are spraying an endless stream of independent photon bullets outward from your device. The photons hitting the left side of the Moon arrived from Earth, and the photons hitting the right side arrived a moment later from another burst.'),
 ('S13', 'Shadows in Deep Space', 'ill', 'p07a', 'We see a very similar trick when we examine the physics of shadows. Imagine standing in front of a powerful spotlight in a vast auditorium, casting your shadow onto a giant wall far behind you. If you slowly raise your hand, your shadow rises across the wall.'),
 ('S14', 'Shadows in Deep Space', 'ill', 'p07b', 'Now, imagine that the back wall is moved light-years away into deep space, onto a massive interstellar dust cloud. If you move your fingers in front of the lamp, the shadow cast onto that cosmic cloud would sweep across vast light-years in mere seconds.'),
 ('S15', 'Giant Scissors', 'ill', 'p08a', 'Consider another classic thought experiment involving a giant pair of scissors. Picture a pair of blades that stretch across entire astronomical units, extending far past the orbit of Mars. As you slowly close the handles on Earth, the point where the two sharp edges intersect begins to slide outward along the blades.'),
 ('S16', 'Giant Scissors', 'ill', 'p08b', 'If the angle between the two blades is infinitesimally small, that point of intersection will race forward toward the tips at a velocity far greater than the speed of light. Does this mean the intersection point breaks the cosmic limit?'),
 ('S17', 'The Intersection Point', 'ill', 'p09a', 'Physicists will tell you that the intersection point is an abstract mathematical concept, not a material object. There is no physical particle moving along the blades; it is simply a geometric location where two pieces of metal overlap in space.'),
 ('S18', 'The Intersection Point', 'ill', 'p09b', 'Furthermore, if you close the handles on Earth, the mechanical force must travel through the atoms of the blades via electromagnetic interactions. The blades themselves would bend, transmitting the motion through sound waves in the steel, which travel tens of thousands of times slower than light. The tips would not even begin to move until more than a year later.'),
 ('S19', 'Light in Water', 'chalk', 'p10a', 'In pure water, for instance, light travels at approximately 225,000 kilometers per second, which is about three-quarters of its maximum speed in empty space. The electromagnetic fields of the water molecules interact with the passing light waves, creating a cumulative delay that slows down the wave front.'),
 ('S20', 'Light in Water', 'ill', 'p10b', 'Now, imagine a high-energy particle, such as an electron ejected from a decaying radioactive fuel rod, entering that same pool of water. That energetic electron might plunge into the water traveling at 250,000 kilometers per second.'),
 ('S21', 'Cherenkov Radiation', 'ill', 'p11a', 'In that specific environment, the electron is moving faster than the local speed of light in water. What happens when a physical particle outpaces light within a physical medium? The result is one of the most stunning sights in nuclear physics, known as Cherenkov radiation.'),
 ('S22', 'Cherenkov Radiation', 'ill', 'p11b', 'When an aircraft travels faster than the speed of sound in air, it creates an acoustic shock wave, a booming pressure front that shakes the ground. Similarly, when a charged particle travels faster than the phase velocity of light in a medium, it generates an optical shock wave.'),
 ('S23', 'Overtaking Slow Light', 'ill', 'p12a', 'Cherenkov radiation proves that the true cosmic speed limit is not simply how fast light happens to be moving inside a room or a glass of water. The true limit is the invariant constant c, the speed of light in the pure vacuum of space.'),
 ('S24', 'Overtaking Slow Light', 'ill', 'p12b', 'The electron in the nuclear pool did not break the universal speed limit of 299,792 kilometers per second; it merely overtook light that had been slowed down by a crowd of molecules. But waves hide an even subtler trick.'),
 ('S25', 'Phase and Group Velocity', 'ill', 'p13a', 'To understand how waves behave, physicists often distinguish between two different concepts: phase velocity and group velocity. Imagine standing on an ocean pier watching long rolling swells travel toward the shore.'),
 ('S26', 'Phase and Group Velocity', 'ill', 'p13b', 'If you look closely at the individual ripples traveling along the crest of a larger wave, you might notice that those tiny ripples seem to race ahead, appearing at the back of the swell and disappearing at the front.'),
 ('S27', 'Phase and Group Velocity', 'ill', 'p13c', 'The speed of those individual crests is the phase velocity, while the speed of the overall wave packet carrying energy is the group velocity. Here is the strange part.'),
 ('S28', 'Phase and Group Velocity', 'ill', 'p13d', 'In some materials, the crests of a light wave can have a phase velocity greater than c. Yet nothing real rides along with them, and a pattern of crests carries no message. The signal itself, the front of the wave, never outruns c.'),
 ('S29', 'Entangled Particles', 'ill', 'p14a', 'If waves and optical illusions cannot carry messages faster than light, what about the strange and mysterious world of quantum mechanics? This is where even Einstein himself felt deeply unsettled.'),
 ('S30', 'Entangled Particles', 'ill', 'p14b', 'In the quantum realm, particles can become entangled, meaning their physical properties become inextricably linked regardless of the distance separating them. Imagine creating a pair of entangled particles in a laboratory, keeping one particle on Earth, and sending the other particle to an astronaut near the rings of Saturn.'),
 ('S31', 'Instant Correlation', 'ill', 'p15a', 'According to quantum mechanics, until you measure those particles, their states remain fundamentally undetermined, existing in a cloud of probabilities known as a superposition. If one particle spins upward, the other must spin downward, but neither particle has chosen a definite orientation yet.'),
 ('S32', 'Instant Correlation', 'ill', 'p15b', 'In the standard description, the moment you measure the particle on Earth and find that its spin is pointing up, the particle near Saturn is instantly fixed in the opposite state, pointing down.'),
 ('S33', 'Instant Correlation', 'ill', 'p15c', 'The link appears instantaneous, seemingly thousands or millions of times faster than light could ever bridge the distance. Yet physicists discovered something profound: entanglement cannot be used to send any useful information faster than light.'),
 ('S34', 'The No-Communication Theorem', 'ill', 'p16a', 'This ironclad rule is known in physics as the No-Communication Theorem. Imagine the astronaut near Saturn looking at their particle. Because the outcomes of quantum measurements are fundamentally random, the astronaut will simply see an unpredictable sequence of spin-up and spin-down results.'),
 ('S35', 'The No-Communication Theorem', 'ill', 'p16b', 'They have no way of knowing whether you have performed a measurement on Earth or not. To decode any meaningful message, the astronaut must wait for you to send a classical message, via radio waves or light signals, telling them which measurements you made. That classical signal can only travel at the speed of light.'),
 ('S36', 'No Cosmic Telegraph', 'ill', 'p17a', 'Nature seems to protect causality with remarkable elegance. Entanglement provides an instantaneous correlation between two distant points in space, but it adamantly refuses to act as a cosmic telegraph. But if the laws of physics prevent particles, waves, and quantum states from outrunning light, what about the stage upon which all of these things exist? What about space itself?'),
 ('S37', 'Receding Galaxies', 'ill', 'p18a', 'When we turn our telescopes toward the farthest reaches of the night sky, we encounter the most staggering realization of all. The universe is filled with billions of galaxies, and almost all of them are moving away from us. In the early twentieth century, astronomers realized that the farther away a galaxy is, the faster it appears to be receding.'),
 ('S38', 'Receding Galaxies', 'chalk', 'p18b', 'For the most distant galaxies visible in our deep-space observatories, their apparent recession speed is not just half the speed of light, or ninety percent of the speed of light. They are moving away from us faster than light itself.'),
 ('S39', 'Receding Galaxies', 'ill', 'p18c', 'How can that be? The speed limit applies to objects moving through space, not to the stretching of space itself.'),
 ('S40', 'The Raisin Bread Universe', 'ill', 'p19a', 'Imagine baking a loaf of raisin bread in your kitchen oven. As the dough warms and rises, it expands uniformly in every direction. If you observe two raisins that were initially close together, they move apart slowly as the dough stretches between them.'),
 ('S41', 'The Raisin Bread Universe', 'ill', 'p19b', 'But if you observe two raisins located on opposite ends of the loaf, the amount of dough expanding between them is far greater. From the perspective of either raisin, the other seems to be flying away at a tremendous speed, even though neither raisin is actually crawling through the bread.'),
 ('S42', 'Stretching Space', 'ill', 'p20a', 'In our cosmos, the galaxies are like those raisins, and spacetime itself is the expanding dough. Galaxies are not rocketing through the void. Instead, the space between them keeps stretching.'),
 ('S43', 'Stretching Space', 'ill', 'p20b', 'Because the expansion of space adds up across vast cosmic distances, the cumulative rate of expansion across billions of light-years easily outpaces the speed of light. The light emitted by those distant galaxies is stretched into longer, redder wavelengths, eventually fading into the deep infrared.'),
 ('S44', 'The Event Horizon', 'ill', 'p21a', 'This brings us to a poignant boundary known as the cosmological event horizon. In the standard model of cosmology, galaxies beyond a certain distance are carried away so fast that the light they emit today will never be able to reach our eyes.'),
 ('S45', 'The Event Horizon', 'ill', 'p21b', 'It is as if the galaxy is trying to swim upstream against a cosmic river that flows faster than its swimming stroke. Over hundreds of billions of years, those distant beacons will slip silently out of view, leaving our local group of galaxies isolated in the darkness.'),
 ('S46', 'Why It Matters', 'ill', 'p22a', 'Every road in physics leads us back to the same profound realization. The universal speed limit is not an obstacle created to frustrate human ambition; it is the fundamental architecture that makes our universe stable, orderly, and knowable.'),
 ('S47', 'Why It Matters', 'ill', 'p22b', 'If you could travel faster than light in a vacuum, you would inevitably violate causality. Under the mathematics of special relativity, if you can send a signal faster than light in one reference frame, there always exists another reference frame where that signal arrives before it was even sent.'),
 ('S48', 'Tonight', 'ill', 'p23a', 'Tonight, as you look around your home or gaze up at the quiet stars through your window, take a moment to appreciate the invisible boundaries that hold reality together.'),
 ('S49', 'Tonight', 'ill', 'p23b', 'The light from your bedside lamp, the faint glow of your smartphone screen, and the photons streaming from galaxies billions of years old are all honoring the exact same speed limit. That steady, invariant speed is the silent heartbeat of the cosmos, preserving the order of cause and effect across every second of our lives.'),
]
